Chapter: FieldRangeOverflow

 The adversarial half of field-range-proven: the lint's field arm must
 DERIVE the range, not wave a field access through.

 b.val / 8 over 0..255 is 0..31, which does not fit a 0..15 Nibble.
 An arm that merely noticed "the receiver is bounded" and silenced the
 store would accept this. The arm computes the quotient interval, so
 the store is still rejected.

 This is also where the diagnostic's NUMBER is recorded, because the
 harness checks codes and not message text, and the code here is the one
 every unproven store raises. The error must read `0..31` -- the proven
 range the verdict was reached on. It read `0..255` for a while, quoting
 the inferred type instead: neither the bound nor the proof. Read it by
 eye; a sidecar cannot.

Section: Definitions

  Byte = record {
   val : Integer between 0 and 255
  }

  Nibble = record {
   n : Integer between 0 and 15
  }

  eighth : Byte -> Integer
  eighth (b) = (Nibble { n = b.val / 8 }).n

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (integer-to-text (eighth (Byte { val = 200 })))
  end
